  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'Option A, because there one has a higher probability of making a profit (or winning).' The individual explicitly considers the probability of obtaining a nonzero (positive) outcome — that is, 'making a profit.' They note that Lottery A offers a higher chance of winning something compared to Lottery B. The justification is based purely on the likelihood of a nonzero gain, not on the magnitude of the outcomes or their full distribution. The focus is on the chance of avoiding a zero outcome, which is framed as a 'win.'",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness — specifically, whether favorable outcomes are more densely probable. This aligns with the idea that a higher probability on better outcomes (even if smaller in magnitude) makes a lottery more left-skewed. The individual’s consideration of the 'higher probability of making a profit' corresponds to comparing the likelihood of nonzero outcomes: 50% in A vs. 25% in B. This is a key component of skewness in this context. While the individual does not mention the full concept of skewness or distribution shape explicitly, their reasoning focuses on the same underlying feature — the concentration of probability on favorable (non-zero) outcomes. The justification matches the directional logic of the decision reason, which prefers the more left-skewed (i.e., higher chance of a positive outcome) lottery. Therefore, the individual’s reasoning is consistent with the decision reason, even if not articulated in technical terms.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
}

{
